	abstract = {Over the past few years, there has been a trend toward increasing levels of warehouse automation, especially in large e-commerce distribution centers. Our subject of interest is robotic sorting systems, in which autonomous or centrally controlled mobile robots are used for automatic sorting. The report examines several autonomous and centralized approaches to solving problems using mobile robots for mail sorting centers. The Ant Colony Optimization (ACO) algorithm was modified to run a swarm to deliver a large set of mail pieces with different types of mail delivery target distributions. ACO algorithms are compared with a traditional centralized robot control system based on Dijkstra's approach. It is shown that centralized algorithms have approximately twofold superiority in performance on the selected 9×9 and 12×12 model warehouses. At the same time, the complexity of centralized management algorithms leads to the need to use modern high-performance systems and imposes restrictions on the use of simulation methods to build higher-level postal logistics management solutions. © 2024 IEEE.},
