	abstract = {Processes related to the automation and digitization of warehouse systems intensified after 2020. The reasons for this can be found in two areas: (1) the development of the Logistics 4.0 concept supported by the popularization of tools provided by Industry 4.0 and (2) the effects of the COVID-19 pandemic, particularly disruptions and delays in logistics flows caused by employee absenteeism. Managers see automation as a solution to eliminating human errors that reduce the company's logistics service level. However, automation does not eliminate humans from logistics processes but only changes their roles. This means that instead of errors made by a single contractor, systemic errors are made by the person planning and monitoring the entire process. For this reason, it is crucial to identify potential adverse events and assess the risk of their occurrence to implement preventive solutions. The article aims to present the research results on analyzing adverse events in the picking process at a selected manufacturer. The tests were conducted in a real system - in a warehouse of finished products. The research aimed to identify adverse events caused by human errors or the functioning of the automatic load-handling system. For this reason, the measurements were carried out for the traditional picking process (person-to-goods method) and after introducing an automatic storage system (goods-to-person system). © The Author(s), under exclusive license to Springer Nature Switzerland AG 2023.},
