	affiliations = {Henan University, Zhengzhou, China},
	abstract = {Domestic mainstream e-commerce companies have begun to introduce AGVs (Automated Guided Vehicles) into warehouses, but how to reasonably batch orders and schedule tasks to minimize operating and time costs has become a huge challenge for them to build intelligent warehouses. First, this paper treats two batching principles to combine original orders into wave orders, and then verifies the effectiveness of the model under different objectives with the help of optimization software CPLEX. By testing the case on a largest scale of 3 picking stations, 10 mobile shelves, 10 initial orders, and 8 products categories, the experimental results show that: 1) both order batching save picking time and outbound times compared to original orders. Under Initial-in-Batch principle, the number of wave orders is less, and the optimization effect is obvious. Under Accumulative-in-Batch principle, the similarity of each range of wave orders is higher, but the optimization effect is minor.2) when the optimization goal is outbound times, the number can be effectively reduced, thereby reducing the total walking time of the AGVs, which is better than picking time-based optimization goal. The research can help companies improve picking efficiency and reduce operating costs, provide scientific support for business application of Parts-to-picker system, and promote the construction of domestic intelligent warehouses. © 2021 ACM.},
