	volume = {2018-December},
	pages = {},
	url = {https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-85061311195&partnerID=40&md5=9ece90d61a0b4a76eda47e6aab9e7a01},
	affiliations = {Victoria University of Wellington, School of Management, Wellington, WGN, New Zealand; ContainerCo, Wellington, New Zealand},
	abstract = {Introduction-This paper aims to answer the question: Can the concept of lean operations, perhaps in conjunction with the use of automated gantry cranes, improve efficiencies in empty container depots? Literature Review-A review of the literature around empty container depots revealed that while lean operations have been applied to manufacturing, warehousing, and ports, it has not yet been applied to empty container depots. Whereas warehouses primarily deal with stock-holding and ports deal with quayside operations, empty container depots act as a conduit between consignors and consignees. Methodology and Context-A New Zealand company that handles roughly half a million TEUs per annum cooperated with this study. A positivist approach was adopted. The study was conducted by analysing data from three depots (Auckland, Tauranga, Napier) selected to illustrate variations in container volumes, types of flows (imports, exports, seasonality), and depot layouts. Data Analysis-The current operations of these depots were analysed using lean operations and value stream mapping. The results of the analysis were then used to develop an optimal framework in which waste was identified and eliminated, primarily via the application of automated gantry cranes. We named this framework the “Ideal Cube” as it demonstrates how lean operations and automated gantry cranes minimise excess handling, and maximise synchronised flows and land utilisation. Discussion-A key finding was that efficiency gains differ from site to site based on their unique demands and overall layouts. Furthermore, changes towards the “Ideal Cube” and gantry cranes need to be implemented incrementally based on continuous improvement. Conclusion-The practical applications of this research case are scalable on a vast level. Future research will enhance the robustness of the “Ideal Cube” by applying it to a greater variety of empty container depots, and by the use of mathematical modelling. © 2018, Curran Associates Inc. All rights reserved.},
	author_keywords = {Automated gantry cranes; Business case; Empty container depots; Lean operations; Logistics and supply chain management},
