	affiliations = {Ajou University, Department of Industrial Engineering, Suwon, Gyeonggi-do, South Korea},
	abstract = {In many distribution centers, a large volume of products is received from many suppliers every day. In some cases such as the apparel industry, the quantity of product in a box may not be equal to that marked on the box due to human errors in the supplier's order picking process. It takes too much time to open every box to check the quantity of products in the box at the inbound inspection station; and any quantity discrepancy found later in the distribution process often results in substantial time delays and cost. Automatic quantity inspection by weighing can be a viable alternative for this problem. In this paper, we review the methods to check the quantity of product in a box by weighing; and present a cost minimization model, in which the decision variable is the acceptance range. An approximate solution procedure is proposed, and its average performance is examined. We also present an acceptance range for outbound weight-based quantity inspection. © 2012 International Information Institute.},
