	affiliations = {Lockheed Martin Space Systems Company, Boulevard, CO, United States; Human Spaceflight-Project Orion, Denver, CO, United States; Human Spaceflight-Project Orion, Denver, CO, United States; Human Spaceflight-Project Orion, Denver, CO, United States; Human Spaceflight-Project Orion, Denver, CO, United States},
	abstract = {Lockheed Martin Corporation and industry partners - with grants from the States of Texas and Colorado - are investing in Exploration Testbeds to be used by industry and NASA to conduct early avionics and software risk mitigation activities for Project Orion and the Constellation Programs. Although dedicated Avionics System Integration Labs have been built in the past and are planned for Constellation, this capability has been designed to support avionics and software risk mitigation much earlier in the design phase. The Exploration Testbeds consist of geographically distributed test and verification facilities connected via an extranet, an integrated test automation framework tool suite, and a shared data warehouse. This paper will discuss the historical importance of System Integration Labs, provide an overview of the Exploration Testbeds, review progress on the build up of this capability, and summarize testing to date. Copyright © 2009 by the American Institute of Aeronautics and Astronautics, Inc.},
